    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "In the figure (where $BH\\perp AC$ and $CH\\perp AB$), $B\\hat HC$ is equal to",
    "image": "qimages/samf/senior-1997-r1/q07.webp",
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "$3\\hat A$"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "$360^\\circ-\\hat A$"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "$180^\\circ-\\hat A$"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "$2\\hat A$"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "$\\hat A$"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "C",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Use the right-angled triangles",
        "body": "Let the altitude from $C$ meet $AB$ at $F$ and the altitude from $B$ meet $AC$ at $E$. In the right triangle $AFC$, $\\angle ACF=90^\\circ-\\hat A$. Then $\\angle BHC$ is an exterior angle of the right triangle $HEC$ (right angle at $E$), so $\\angle BHC=90^\\circ+\\angle HCE=90^\\circ+(90^\\circ-\\hat A)=180^\\circ-\\hat A$.\n\n**Answer: (C) $180^\\circ-\\hat A$**"
      },
      {
        "title": "Quadrilateral $AFHE$",
        "body": "In quadrilateral $AFHE$ the angles at $F$ and $E$ are $90^\\circ$, so $\\angle FHE=360^\\circ-180^\\circ-\\hat A=180^\\circ-\\hat A$. This equals the vertically opposite angle $\\angle BHC$.\n\n**Answer: (C)**"
      }
    ],
    "topics": [
      "geometry"
    ],
    "difficulty": 2,
    "duplicateOf": null,
    "reviewNote": null
  },
